; ============================================================
; TITLE:    The Classic Recursive Tree
; CATEGORY: Classic Logo
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     recursion, trees, state transparency, random
; SUMMARY:  The recursive tree every Logo book draws, in three versions: plain, grown-up with thick trunk and leaves, and a wind-blown random one.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   Draws three trees side by side. The first is the plain textbook
;   tree: every branch splits into two smaller branches. The second has
;   a thick brown trunk that thins out and green leaves on the tips.
;   The third uses random angles and lengths, so it looks more natural
;   and is different every time.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run. Run again to get a new wild tree. Type  clean tree 80
;   in the command center to draw the plain tree on its own.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * a procedure that uses itself on a smaller problem
;   * the stop rule that keeps the recursion from going on forever
;   * "state transparency": each branch puts the turtle back exactly
;     where it found it, which is why the pieces fit together
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Change the 30-degree turns to 20 or 45 in TREE.
;   * Make the branches shrink by 0.6 or 0.8 instead of 0.7.
;   * Give WILD.TREE three branches instead of two.
; ============================================================

; This tree, in one form or another, appears in nearly every Logo book,
; including Abelson and diSessa's Turtle Geometry and Harvey's Computer
; Science Logo Style. The important rule, stressed in both, is that the
; procedure must leave the turtle in the same place and heading it
; started with. Then a big tree is just a trunk with two smaller trees
; on top, and each of those can trust the other to tidy up after itself.

to tree :size
  if :size < 5 [stop]
  forward :size
  left 30
  tree :size * 0.7
  right 60
  tree :size * 0.7
  left 30
  back :size          ; go back down the trunk: state transparency
end

; The same shape, but the pen gets thinner with the size, and tiny
; branches get a green leaf dot.
to grown.tree :size
  if :size < 6 [
    setpencolor [60 170 70]
    setpensize 7
    dot pos
    stop
  ]
  setpencolor [110 70 40]
  setpensize :size / 7
  forward :size
  left 25
  grown.tree :size * 0.72
  right 50
  grown.tree :size * 0.72
  left 25
  ; reset the pen, because the branches changed it on the way
  setpencolor [110 70 40]
  setpensize :size / 7
  penup back :size pendown
end

; Random turns and lengths, and a color that goes from brown at the
; trunk to green at the twigs. Because we remember the exact angles we
; used, we can undo them, so the tree is still state transparent.
to wild.tree :size
  if :size < 5 [stop]
  localmake "a 10 + random 25
  localmake "b 10 + random 25
  setpensize :size / 10
  setpencolor (list 60 + :size / 2 110 - :size / 3 40)
  forward :size
  left :a
  wild.tree :size * (60 + random 25) / 100
  right :a + :b
  wild.tree :size * (60 + random 25) / 100
  left :b
  penup back :size pendown
end

to plant :x :kind :size
  penup setxy :x -250 setheading 0 pendown
  run (list :kind :size)
end

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground [235 242 230]
setpensize 2
setpencolor [70 70 70]
plant -270 "tree 62
plant 0 "grown.tree 82
plant 270 "wild.tree 80
